# Target C — Section 126: full-core strict rhombi localize to harmonic cells up to o(D)

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**
**Status:** proof-only research candidate on theorem-state `2026-09-12.audit.46`.
**Dependencies:** canonical Sections 75, 82, 83 and proof-only Section 124.

Section 124 proves that, in the Type-II branch and for all sufficiently large survivors,
there are no nonconsecutive loaded-loaded unit contacts:

\[
\boxed{c_B=0.}
\]

This removes the only linear-scale ambiguity in an older proof-only localization
argument. Let `R_4` be the number of bounded strict-rhombus faces of the full
nonisolated positive-contact core. For each unloaded harmonic component `I_r`, let
`H_r` be the canonical induced harmonic graph on `I_r union A_r`, and let `R_r` be
its number of bounded quadrilateral faces. Then

\[
\boxed{
0\le R_4-\sum_rR_r\le 3c_I+5
=O(D^{2/3})=o(D).
}
\tag{126.1}
\]

Thus all but `o(D)` full-core strict rhombi occur in exactly one harmonic cell. If
`A_4` is the total area of all full-core strict rhombi and `A_{4,r}` is the local
strict-rhombus area in `H_r`, then also

\[
\boxed{
0\le A_4-\sum_rA_{4,r}\le R_4-\sum_rR_r=o(D).
}
\tag{126.2}
\]

The area bound in (126.2) uses the correct universal estimate
`area(unit rhombus)<=1`. An earlier proof-only draft incorrectly wrote
`sqrt(3)/2`; a unit square is the counterexample to that sentence. The asymptotic
conclusion was unaffected, and is repaired here.

## 126.1 Every local quadrilateral is a physical full-core rhombus

Canonical Section 82 makes every bounded quadrilateral face of `H_r` a strict unit
rhombus. We first check that it is also a bounded face of the full positive-contact
core.

Let `Q` be such a local rhombus. Both diagonals of `Q` are at least one by source
separation. Equality of either diagonal would create a unit equilateral triangle with
two adjacent unit sides, so triangle-freeness gives both diagonals strictly greater
than one. For a unit rhombus the squared diagonals sum to four, hence each
half-diagonal is strictly less than one.

Split `Q` at its center into four triangles. In each center triangle, the unit rhombus
side is a longest side and the two center-to-vertex sides are strictly shorter than
one. Therefore every point in the relative interior of the triangle is strictly less
than one from at least one endpoint of its unit side. Unit separation excludes every
omitted source point from the interior of `Q`. A source point cannot lie in the
relative interior of a unit side either, since it would be less than one from an
endpoint.

A contact edge of the full core cannot properly cross a rhombus side in the straight
unit-contact embedding. Nor can an omitted edge cut the rhombus interior between two
boundary vertices: `H_r` is induced on `I_r union A_r`, and the only possible
diagonals are nonunit. Hence the local four-cycle bounds an actual full-core face.

No physical rhombus is counted in two distinct harmonic cells. If it has an unloaded
vertex, that vertex determines its unloaded component. If all four vertices are
loaded, duplicate counting would put the same four loaded vertices in two distinct
attachment sets, contradicting canonical Section 83's common-attachment restriction.
Thus

\[
\sum_rR_r\le R_4.
\tag{126.3}
\]

Put

\[
\Delta_R:=R_4-\sum_rR_r\ge0.
\]

## 126.2 Classification of a missing full-core strict rhombus

Let `u-v-w-x-u` be a strict-rhombus face of the full positive-contact core which is
not a quadrilateral face of any harmonic graph. Classify it by the number of loaded
vertices among its four cycle vertices.

### Zero or one loaded vertex

The unloaded vertices are joined around the face by a unit-contact path, so they lie
in one unloaded component. Every loaded cycle vertex is adjacent by a unit contact to
that component and hence is one of its attachments. All four face vertices therefore
belong to one induced harmonic graph. Since the full-core face interior is empty, the
rhombus is a local quadrilateral face, contradiction.

Thus no missing rhombus has zero or one loaded vertex.

### Two adjacent loaded vertices

The two unloaded vertices are adjacent by a unit contact, so they lie in one unloaded
component, and both loaded vertices attach to that component. Again the full-core face
is a local quadrilateral face. Hence this case contributes nothing.

### Two opposite loaded vertices

If the two unloaded vertices belong to the same component, the same argument makes the
rhombus local. A missing rhombus therefore has its two unloaded vertices in distinct
components `I_r,I_s` and its two opposite loaded vertices `a,b` as attachments of both
components.

Both convex attachment cells `P_r,P_s` contain the segment `ab`. Canonical Section 83
gives pairwise disjoint cell interiors. Consequently the relative interior of `ab`
cannot lie in the interior of either cell; it is a side of both cells, with the cells
on opposite sides.

A fixed pair of components cannot produce two different shared loaded pairs: two
distinct pairs would give at least three common attachments, forbidden by Section 83.
For a fixed loaded pair `a,b`, the other two vertices of a unit rhombus are the two
intersection points of the unit circles centered at `a,b`, so at most one such rhombus
exists.

Therefore these missing rhombi inject into positive-length side adjacencies of the
interior-disjoint convex cells. Their adjacency graph is planar: choose one dual point
inside each cell and route one dual edge across each shared side. Thus this class has
at most

\[
3c_I
\tag{126.4}
\]

members, using the coarse planar bound valid also for the finite small-`c_I` cases.

### Three loaded vertices

Write the cycle as `u-v-w-x-u`, where `x` is unloaded. The component of `x` has `u`
and `w` as attachments. If `v` were also an attachment, all four face vertices would
belong to one harmonic graph and the rhombus would be local. Hence a missing rhombus
has `v` absent from that attachment set.

The two sides `uv` and `vw` are loaded-loaded unit contacts. Proof-only Section 124
therefore makes both of them consecutive sides of the global loaded force hull. The
rhombus lies on the inward side of those supporting edges, so its angle at `v` is the
global hull interior angle there.

If this angle is `theta`, the two rhombus diagonals have lengths
`2 sin(theta/2)` and `2 cos(theta/2)`. Both are strictly greater than one, so

\[
\frac\pi3<\theta<\frac{2\pi}3.
\]

Thus the exterior hull turn at `v` is strictly greater than `pi/3`. Total convex
turning is `2pi`, so fewer than six loaded hull vertices can support such a rhombus.
For a fixed central hull vertex `v`, its two hull neighbors are fixed and determine at
most one fourth rhombus vertex. Hence this class has at most

\[
5
\tag{126.5}
\]

members.

### Four loaded vertices

Every side of the rhombus is now a loaded-loaded unit contact, hence by Section 124 a
global hull side. If all four cycle sides are global hull sides, those four vertices
already form the entire cyclic hull boundary. This forces `q=4`, impossible for all
sufficiently large Type-II survivors, where `q=Omega(D)`.

Thus the all-loaded missing class is empty for large `D`.

## 126.3 The localization bound

Combining the four cases gives the finite large-Type-II estimate

\[
\boxed{
0\le\Delta_R\le3c_I+5.
}
\tag{126.6}
\]

Canonical Section 75 gives

\[
c_I=O(D^{2/3}),
\]

so (126.1) follows.

For the area statement, every unit rhombus of side length one has area

\[
\sin\theta\le1.
\]

The area difference `A_4-sum_r A_{4,r}` is exactly the sum of areas of the missing
full-core rhombi. Therefore

\[
0\le A_4-\sum_rA_{4,r}
\le\Delta_R=o(D),
\]

proving (126.2).

## 126.4 Scope and use

- The theorem is Type II only because it uses Section 124.
- Contact-isolated source points are retained. The local-face check explicitly rules
  out an omitted source point inside a local rhombus by unit separation; it does not
  add isolated coordinates to the Section-57 affine kernel.
- The theorem concerns physical full-core rhombi, not an abstract quadrangulation.
- The `o(D)` localization loss is stronger than the `O(D)` loaded-touching loss used
  in canonical Section 122.
- No diameter-decreasing competitor is constructed, and C-II remains open.

**Conclusion:** proof-only structural theorem. In the Type-II branch, harmonic-cell
rhombus accounting loses only `o(D)` full-core rhombi, with the corrected area bound
`area<=1`.